#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723048 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "036b3206-5ea2-5a68-ae88-d1a9bdda447c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$10$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723031} {:answer/id #uuid "20ce972e-719f-5f10-bd15-0b425ee1676d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$18$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723035} {:answer/id #uuid "b24e1eaf-5a1c-545c-88aa-074309a628da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$90$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723051} {:answer/id #uuid "b9b79ceb-8a04-5860-92de-9e4558380cf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$180$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723053} {:answer/id #uuid "c16e7000-c36e-549b-b18a-ac9f5877b403" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$45$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723056}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723056} :answer-field/id #uuid "03d73a35-9065-5454-b0a4-c7f5b8741621" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723032}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9d6308fa-c278-5683-8431-c8f521da1a60" :question/problem "The number of students affected by flu $P(t)$ increases toward a maximum of $1300$ students. Time $t\\ge 0$ is measured in days from the start of monitoring. At $t=0$, the quantity is $750$ students. At $t=5$, it is $870$ students. At what time $t$, measured from the start of monitoring, does the quantity reach $1240$ students? Round to the nearest integer.\n\n*Hint: Assume an inhibited growth model.*" :question/worked-solution "The growth rate is proportional to the remaining deficit. Thus\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(1300-P).\n$$\n\nSeparate and integrate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{1300-P}&=k\\,dt \\\\ -\\ln|1300-P|&=kt+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe deficit is positive on this growth branch. Exponentiating and setting $K=e^{-C}>0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1300-P&=e^{-kt-C} \\\\ &=Ke^{-kt} \\\\ P(t)&=1300-Ke^{-kt}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the initial condition:\n\n$$\nK=1300-750=550.\n$$\n\nCalibrate the rate with the later observation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}550e^{-5k}&=430 \\\\ -5k&=\\ln\\left(\\frac{43}{55}\\right) \\\\ k&=\\frac{1}{5}\\ln\\left(\\frac{55}{43}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\nP(t)=1300-550e^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nFor the requested target, solve for the original time $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60&=550e^{-k(t-0)} \\\\ -k(t-0)&=\\ln\\left(\\frac{6}{55}\\right) \\\\ t&=0+\\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{55}{6}\\right)}{k} \\\\ &\\approx 45.007640 \\\\ &\\approx 45.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is approximately $45$ days from the start of monitoring."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723054 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10bd4cad-932f-56d9-b8b0-ab169ccae880" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$37$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723033} {:answer/id #uuid "28df8b48-2039-55b4-9c5f-ed1bcff2709f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$27$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723036} {:answer/id #uuid "6964d0c7-0083-5071-9152-18611aa1cac1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$39$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723041} {:answer/id #uuid "7886e0f3-0a2c-564c-873f-207783d515b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$20$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723045} {:answer/id #uuid "e3dc4d74-a9d1-5aea-a967-16db0af684eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$78$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723059}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723041} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7d83ddc-aff1-528d-ba09-faf97f58d265" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723061}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c08c7e31-b54c-599f-8419-cd4634f8a029" :question/problem "The number of antibiotic-resistant bacteria $P(t)$ increases toward a maximum of $1000$ bacteria. Time $t\\ge 0$ is measured in hours from the start of monitoring. At $t=0$, the quantity is $330$ bacteria. At $t=10$, it is $450$ bacteria. At what time $t$, measured from the start of monitoring, does the quantity reach $690$ bacteria? Round to the nearest integer.\n\n*Hint: Assume an inhibited growth model.*" :question/worked-solution "The growth rate is proportional to the remaining deficit. Thus\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(1000-P).\n$$\n\nSeparate and integrate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{1000-P}&=k\\,dt \\\\ -\\ln|1000-P|&=kt+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe deficit is positive on this growth branch. Exponentiating and setting $K=e^{-C}>0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1000-P&=e^{-kt-C} \\\\ &=Ke^{-kt} \\\\ P(t)&=1000-Ke^{-kt}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the initial condition:\n\n$$\nK=1000-330=670.\n$$\n\nCalibrate the rate with the later observation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}670e^{-10k}&=550 \\\\ -10k&=\\ln\\left(\\frac{55}{67}\\right) \\\\ k&=\\frac{1}{10}\\ln\\left(\\frac{67}{55}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\nP(t)=1000-670e^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nFor the requested target, solve for the original time $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}310&=670e^{-k(t-0)} \\\\ -k(t-0)&=\\ln\\left(\\frac{31}{67}\\right) \\\\ t&=0+\\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{67}{31}\\right)}{k} \\\\ &\\approx 39.050852 \\\\ &\\approx 39.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is approximately $39$ hours from the start of monitoring."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723055 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31ca8ed2-8d31-593c-99a9-4219517974f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$67$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723037} {:answer/id #uuid "3a60e399-31dc-5bc8-a571-b34f513c0ec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$26$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723038} {:answer/id #uuid "b4a2c559-6bcf-58c0-9f7f-9dee1194cc96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$17$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723052} {:answer/id #uuid "c32b4fd4-e3c1-55fa-abdf-414ae0c5ab7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$33$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723057} {:answer/id #uuid "e6311938-0ab6-5271-bfee-8fb7a2874640" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$21$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723060}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723057} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9190345-9b04-574b-8a9d-c90b88038ef5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c0f1ca2a-b100-5baf-ad60-6630789a76a4" :question/problem "The number of infected deer $P(t)$ increases toward a maximum of $1500$ deer. Time $t\\ge 0$ is measured in days from the start of monitoring. At $t=0$, the quantity is $500$ deer. At $t=10$, it is $770$ deer. At what time $t$, measured from the start of monitoring, does the quantity reach $1150$ deer? Round to the nearest integer.\n\n*Hint: Assume an inhibited growth model.*" :question/worked-solution "The growth rate is proportional to the remaining deficit. Thus\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(1500-P).\n$$\n\nSeparate and integrate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{1500-P}&=k\\,dt \\\\ -\\ln|1500-P|&=kt+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe deficit is positive on this growth branch. Exponentiating and setting $K=e^{-C}>0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1500-P&=e^{-kt-C} \\\\ &=Ke^{-kt} \\\\ P(t)&=1500-Ke^{-kt}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the initial condition:\n\n$$\nK=1500-500=1000.\n$$\n\nCalibrate the rate with the later observation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1000e^{-10k}&=730 \\\\ -10k&=\\ln\\left(\\frac{73}{100}\\right) \\\\ k&=\\frac{1}{10}\\ln\\left(\\frac{100}{73}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\nP(t)=1500-1000e^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nFor the requested target, solve for the original time $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}350&=1000e^{-k(t-0)} \\\\ -k(t-0)&=\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{20}\\right) \\\\ t&=0+\\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{20}{7}\\right)}{k} \\\\ &\\approx 33.358318 \\\\ &\\approx 33.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is approximately $33$ days from the start of monitoring."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723062 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18228a32-326f-5616-905d-1f65689e68ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$13$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723034} {:answer/id #uuid "4bd9ea02-acc4-5f07-9a56-1b4f0a22cf85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$16$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723040} {:answer/id #uuid "6b572fbc-14c5-58eb-aeff-3289eefaa8b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$133$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723042} {:answer/id #uuid "8653cf8e-6a64-59ad-80ba-c3ba107e9a2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$67$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723046} {:answer/id #uuid "fbb404f4-c9de-5375-8d34-8e47db0b86d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$33$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723064}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723064} :answer-field/id #uuid "f913ea85-2c78-52a9-8ca1-7466b4e549a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723063}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e8e42a1e-5234-5923-92d2-6de0f81a0c51" :question/problem "The number of infected deer $P(t)$ increases toward a maximum of $1500$ deer. Time $t\\ge 0$ is measured in days from the start of monitoring. At $t=0$, the quantity is $440$ deer. At $t=9$, it is $940$ deer. At what time $t$, measured from the start of monitoring, does the quantity reach $1400$ deer? Round to the nearest integer.\n\n*Hint: Assume an inhibited growth model.*" :question/worked-solution "The growth rate is proportional to the remaining deficit. Thus\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(1500-P).\n$$\n\nSeparate and integrate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{1500-P}&=k\\,dt \\\\ -\\ln|1500-P|&=kt+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe deficit is positive on this growth branch. Exponentiating and setting $K=e^{-C}>0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1500-P&=e^{-kt-C} \\\\ &=Ke^{-kt} \\\\ P(t)&=1500-Ke^{-kt}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the initial condition:\n\n$$\nK=1500-440=1060.\n$$\n\nCalibrate the rate with the later observation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1060e^{-9k}&=560 \\\\ -9k&=\\ln\\left(\\frac{28}{53}\\right) \\\\ k&=\\frac{1}{9}\\ln\\left(\\frac{53}{28}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\nP(t)=1500-1060e^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nFor the requested target, solve for the original time $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100&=1060e^{-k(t-0)} \\\\ -k(t-0)&=\\ln\\left(\\frac{5}{53}\\right) \\\\ t&=0+\\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{53}{5}\\right)}{k} \\\\ &\\approx 33.299021 \\\\ &\\approx 33.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is approximately $33$ days from the start of monitoring."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723067 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d1c04ab-f970-57c8-a528-355ac1e577df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$5$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723069} {:answer/id #uuid "37dca17e-46fc-5101-add3-ff5cfa9e99a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$21$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723071} {:answer/id #uuid "6d669dbe-df55-5186-a09a-cc18808efed2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$18$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723073} {:answer/id #uuid "a2329d55-2a40-5118-8c14-d35449c2eda0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$37$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723075} {:answer/id #uuid "c09b674b-6044-5446-981a-4a947c5e812f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$9$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723078}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723073} :answer-field/id #uuid "2de876ac-3b6a-5052-9335-47e00f5c7986" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723070}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "28aea038-1308-52b0-9998-5fdfa21c546e" :question/problem "The temperature of a drink $P(t)$, measured in degrees Celsius, obeys\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(30-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\ge 0$ is measured in minutes and $k>0$ is constant. At $t=0$, the quantity is $9$ degrees Celsius. At $t=6$, it is $13$ degrees Celsius. At what time $t$, measured from the start of monitoring, does the quantity reach $19$ degrees Celsius? Round to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "The growth rate is proportional to the remaining deficit. Thus\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(30-P).\n$$\n\nSeparate and integrate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{30-P}&=k\\,dt \\\\ -\\ln|30-P|&=kt+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe deficit is positive on this growth branch. Exponentiating and setting $K=e^{-C}>0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30-P&=e^{-kt-C} \\\\ &=Ke^{-kt} \\\\ P(t)&=30-Ke^{-kt}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the initial condition:\n\n$$\nK=30-9=21.\n$$\n\nCalibrate the rate with the later observation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}21e^{-6k}&=17 \\\\ -6k&=\\ln\\left(\\frac{17}{21}\\right) \\\\ k&=\\frac{1}{6}\\ln\\left(\\frac{21}{17}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\nP(t)=30-21e^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nFor the requested target, solve for the original time $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11&=21e^{-k(t-0)} \\\\ -k(t-0)&=\\ln\\left(\\frac{11}{21}\\right) \\\\ t&=0+\\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{21}{11}\\right)}{k} \\\\ &\\approx 18.360606 \\\\ &\\approx 18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is approximately $18$ minutes from the start of monitoring."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723077 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "611e2f10-8a3f-56c1-89dc-4097c7918cb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$630$ students" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723072} {:answer/id #uuid "9f802549-5a4a-5b57-8df0-5a609dba9fb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$1070$ students" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723074} {:answer/id #uuid "ad63b330-32b9-5324-886f-e8be68090d50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$970$ students" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723076} {:answer/id #uuid "e3759fe6-362e-512a-bf93-516705a633fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$809$ students" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723079} {:answer/id #uuid "eda5be36-4e04-5ff5-a3be-083b32f817a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$989$ students" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723080}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723079} :answer-field/id #uuid "29d3abd5-a925-5764-889e-5c2c131644f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723068}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b0908788-5cdf-5a4c-8957-17e3e270c616" :question/problem "The school population $P(t)$, measured in students, obeys\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.06(1100-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\ge 0$ is measured in years. At $t=0$, the quantity is $340$ students. What is the quantity at $t=16$? Round to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "The growth rate is proportional to the remaining deficit. Thus\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(1100-P).\n$$\n\nSeparate and integrate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{1100-P}&=k\\,dt \\\\ -\\ln|1100-P|&=kt+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe deficit is positive on this growth branch. Exponentiating and setting $K=e^{-C}>0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1100-P&=e^{-kt-C} \\\\ &=Ke^{-kt} \\\\ P(t)&=1100-Ke^{-kt}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the initial condition:\n\n$$\nK=1100-340=760.\n$$\n\nThe supplied rate is $k=0.06$.\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\nP(t)=1100-760e^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nEvaluate using the exact rate, keeping full precision until the final step:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(16)&=1100-760e^{-k16} \\\\ &\\approx 809.001407 \\\\ &\\approx 809.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe quantity is approximately $809$ students."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724699 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28dc683b-1d41-5493-8b6a-e3609145e698" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\frac{dy}{dx}=y+x\\sin\\!\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724696} {:answer/id #uuid "36804a21-fd97-587a-ae02-0b335ec5b1d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+y)\\frac{dy}{dx}=x^2+y^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724697} {:answer/id #uuid "50f3b373-319c-5b7b-8c46-98cbc46de174" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x^2+y^2)\\frac{dy}{dx}=x^2+xy+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724700} {:answer/id #uuid "70b56712-6274-54a0-ba17-5942c8aa5a73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\frac{dy}{dx}=y+x\\sin(y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724702} {:answer/id #uuid "a3e91570-1c5d-5595-b4ce-8416f9c85111" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\frac{dy}{dx}=y+\\sin\\!\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724709}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724696} :answer-field/id #uuid "b38d6291-bea3-5732-8b3b-e614ce524fa1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724711}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4f6f3c97-9fb8-589d-a080-065c6c07348a" :question/problem "For $x>0$ and $y>0$, which of the following is a first-order homogeneous ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "First isolate $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}$ in each equation. The function to test is the entire right-hand side after this rearrangement. It must satisfy $f(tx,ty)=f(x,y)$ for every $t>0$.\n\nThe correct equation is\n\n$$\nx\\frac{dy}{dx}=y+x\\sin\\!\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right).\n$$\n\nDividing by $x>0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y}{x}+\\sin\\!\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right).\n$$\n\nBoth terms depend only on the ratio $y/x$. Directly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(tx,ty)&=\\frac{ty}{tx}+\\sin\\!\\left(\\frac{ty}{tx}\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{y}{x}+\\sin\\!\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\\\\\n&=f(x,y).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe near-miss equations fail for different reasons:\n\n- From $x\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=y+\\sin\\!\\left(\\dfrac{y}{x}\\right)$, we get $f(x,y)=\\dfrac{y}{x}+\\dfrac{1}{x}\\sin\\!\\left(\\dfrac{y}{x}\\right)$. Scaling leaves a factor $1/t$ in the second term. At $(1,\\pi/2)$ and $(2,\\pi)$ the values are $\\pi/2+1$ and $\\pi/2+1/2$.\n- From $(x^2+y^2)\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=x^2+xy+1$, we get $f(x,y)=\\dfrac{x^2+xy+1}{x^2+y^2}$. The constant does not scale with the quadratic terms: $f(1,1)=3/2$, whereas $f(2,2)=9/8$.\n- From $(x+y)\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=x^2+y^2$, we get $f(x,y)=\\dfrac{x^2+y^2}{x+y}$. This has degree $1$, so $f(tx,ty)=t f(x,y)$, not $f(x,y)$ in general.\n- From $x\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=y+x\\sin(y)$, we get $f(x,y)=\\dfrac{y}{x}+\\sin(y)$. The sine term becomes $\\sin(ty)$ under scaling. At $(1,\\pi/2)$ and $(2,\\pi)$ the values are $\\pi/2+1$ and $\\pi/2$.\n\nThus the equation with $y+x\\sin\\!\\left(\\dfrac{y}{x}\\right)$ on its right-hand side is the only homogeneous one. No differential equation needs to be solved."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724704 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10c1cae6-71c2-59a0-980e-0ed49315d7aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{xy^2}{x^2+y^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724695} {:answer/id #uuid "64e08772-2d6e-559b-a20f-93f788e9f87c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x^2+xy}{x+y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724701} {:answer/id #uuid "89696e05-8bab-5812-9ef0-683fd6a62c65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{xy}{x^2+y^2+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724706} {:answer/id #uuid "af983e4b-33a6-52db-9348-fd5b56e250b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x^2+y}{x^2+y^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724710} {:answer/id #uuid "d76e3b85-6504-587e-93eb-8506a55be624" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x^2+xy}{x^2+y^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724714}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724714} :answer-field/id #uuid "8784363a-8015-500f-bdeb-6a3ffad6147e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7c0d40e3-fc78-5cce-93d0-9e04372125d5" :question/problem "For $x>0$ and $y>0$, which of the following is a first-order homogeneous ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "A first-order equation $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=f(x,y)$ is homogeneous when $f(tx,ty)=f(x,y)$ for every $t>0$ on this domain. The right-hand side must have degree zero, not just any degree.\n\n**Method 1: Compare degrees.**\n\nThe correct equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x^2+xy}{x^2+y^2}.\n$$\n\nBoth polynomials are homogeneous of degree $2$: every term has total degree $2$. Their quotient therefore has degree $2-2=0$.\n\n**Method 2: Check scaling directly.**\n\nFor $f(x,y)=\\dfrac{x^2+xy}{x^2+y^2}$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(tx,ty)&=\\frac{(tx)^2+(tx)(ty)}{(tx)^2+(ty)^2}\\\\\n&=\\frac{t^2(x^2+xy)}{t^2(x^2+y^2)}\\\\\n&=f(x,y).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other choices fail the degree-zero test:\n\n- $\\dfrac{x^2+xy}{x+y}$ and $\\dfrac{xy^2}{x^2+y^2}$ both have degree $1$; scaling multiplies each by $t$.\n- $\\dfrac{x^2+y}{x^2+y^2}$ has a degree-$1$ term in its numerator that scales differently. Its values at $(1,1)$ and $(2,2)$ are $1$ and $\\dfrac34$.\n- $\\dfrac{xy}{x^2+y^2+1}$ has a constant term in its denominator. Its values at those same points are $\\dfrac13$ and $\\dfrac49$.\n\nThus only $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{x^2+xy}{x^2+y^2}$ is homogeneous."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724707 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b6a525a-967c-5ed2-a0ba-96034a1bd82e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2xy}{x^2+y^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724698} {:answer/id #uuid "75b26161-d344-5e25-87c3-dc5c0d02d9f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x^2+1}{x^2+xy}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724703} {:answer/id #uuid "9fd12eff-cf0c-502d-889a-62d7f492879b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\sin\\!\\left(\\frac{x+y}{x}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724708} {:answer/id #uuid "caef1fad-3d93-534a-89b3-4b22800f9abf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724713} {:answer/id #uuid "f38f1754-14de-5f9b-9405-2bdd1bc288d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\ln(y)-\\ln(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724715}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724703} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9235c35-c738-5fc7-81fa-e4f4e86b604a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724712}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "92e7bbf9-dedf-5d20-96ca-5fbeb2e0b92e" :question/problem "For $x>0$ and $y>0$, which of the following is **NOT** a first-order homogeneous ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "Write each equation as $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=f(x,y)$. Homogeneity requires $f(tx,ty)=f(x,y)$ for every $t>0$.\n\nThe equation that is **not** homogeneous is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x^2+1}{x^2+xy}.\n$$\n\n**Method 1: Track the constant term.**\n\nScaling both variables gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(tx,ty)&=\\frac{t^2x^2+1}{t^2x^2+t^2xy}\\\\\n&=\\frac{x^2+t^{-2}}{x^2+xy}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe term $t^{-2}$ remains, so this is not the original function for all $t>0$.\n\n**Method 2: Give one counterexample.**\n\nAt $x=y=1$ and $t=2$,\n\n$$\nf(1,1)=1,\\qquad f(2,2)=\\frac58.\n$$\n\nOne failed scaling is enough to disprove homogeneity.\n\nAll four other right-hand sides are unchanged by scaling:\n\n- The logarithm difference equals the logarithm of $y/x$. Scaling leaves this ratio unchanged, so it leaves the difference unchanged.\n- $\\sin\\!\\left(\\dfrac{tx+ty}{tx}\\right)=\\sin\\!\\left(\\dfrac{x+y}{x}\\right)$.\n- $\\dfrac{2(tx)(ty)}{(tx)^2+(ty)^2}=\\dfrac{2xy}{x^2+y^2}$, since the common factor $t^2$ cancels.\n- The constant $3$ is also unchanged; a nonzero constant has degree zero.\n\nTherefore select $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{x^2+1}{x^2+xy}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724902 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35f0e3d0-078f-5861-a32c-6dc4c3c96db0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=216-8y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724901} {:answer/id #uuid "8d8e236e-c93e-561c-aa5f-bc9915cb995d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=1060+20y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724914} {:answer/id #uuid "b6621940-22b5-5a3b-bc30-f5bbf0043989" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-20{y}^{2}+1060y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724923} {:answer/id #uuid "dfdfd320-e41d-56d2-9127-e351b1b8977e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-4{y}^{2}+64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724930} {:answer/id #uuid "e9ef03a3-d9fb-5c46-8462-3deea5fbd914" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=10{y}^{2}-190" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724932}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724901} :answer-field/id #uuid "8eafd7a8-cd50-52e2-8258-feac13a167ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724915}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3c5aefb4-8a98-5360-b427-4b007aa764b8" :question/problem "Given that $y>27$ and $t>0$, which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited decay model has a constant positive proportionality factor and approaches its equilibrium from above:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\\qquad k>0,\\qquad y>a\n$$\n\nRewrite the selected equation without changing its value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dt}&=216-8y \\\\ &=-8(y-27)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $k=8>0$ and the equilibrium is $a=27$. The prompt supplies $y>27$, which places the solution above its equilibrium.\n\nThe remaining alternatives have a positive linear coefficient or a variable factor or power that prevents a model linear in the displacement from equilibrium."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724903 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "206b3ebf-3504-5c40-99db-757c1079ebd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=74+2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724899} {:answer/id #uuid "70dd0bfa-4193-5946-86fd-c7d2ac6fd6d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=74y-2{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724910} {:answer/id #uuid "81626bd0-6f12-575c-ad91-e6743a840565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-4y+116" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724911} {:answer/id #uuid "a22fc3e9-0cde-509c-969a-4328d5204bb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=2{y}^{2}-116" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724919} {:answer/id #uuid "b0a78ca7-fcc6-580f-91dc-ea3a862c2a74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-2{y}^{2}+112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724921}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724911} :answer-field/id #uuid "5269822d-ad1a-5504-9438-9c7c9d48fbe6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3e3575e1-a9b7-589d-9f9c-60e3ee8b64fc" :question/problem "Given that $y>29$ and $t>0$, which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited decay model has a constant positive proportionality factor and approaches its equilibrium from above:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\\qquad k>0,\\qquad y>a\n$$\n\nRewrite the selected equation without changing its value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dt}&=-4y+116 \\\\ &=-4(y-29)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $k=4>0$ and the equilibrium is $a=29$. The prompt supplies $y>29$, which places the solution above its equilibrium.\n\nThe remaining alternatives have a positive linear coefficient or a variable factor or power that prevents a model linear in the displacement from equilibrium."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724917 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4097853b-9113-5547-8651-57d2acbcbce8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=4{y}^{2}-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724904} {:answer/id #uuid "8c20d5c8-0aaa-55ca-a553-0f3688cd4b6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-4{y}^{2}+220y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724913} {:answer/id #uuid "bac2d50d-e350-59e7-aa38-c1cdba08d3c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=220+4y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724924} {:answer/id #uuid "bb25e404-1565-5942-be37-ea32855d5f11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-15y+510" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724925} {:answer/id #uuid "bc63e9fd-4cfe-58a5-93bd-c1a03631215f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-15{y}^{2}+255" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724926}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724925} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9fa1f08-5686-5111-a9b4-154450c6736b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724928}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9a1d0184-b803-5451-a83f-1674a759caa9" :question/problem "Given that $y>34$ and $t>0$, which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited decay model has a constant positive proportionality factor and approaches its equilibrium from above:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\\qquad k>0,\\qquad y>a\n$$\n\nRewrite the selected equation without changing its value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dt}&=-15y+510 \\\\ &=-15(y-34)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $k=15>0$ and the equilibrium is $a=34$. The prompt supplies $y>34$, which places the solution above its equilibrium.\n\nThe remaining alternatives have a positive linear coefficient or a variable factor or power that prevents a model linear in the displacement from equilibrium."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724920 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62f3c07e-e6d0-5210-b478-d9eafa8939d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-20y+860" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724908} {:answer/id #uuid "6b80aa6f-4793-5bd1-a2c1-dc4d2eaca952" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=360y-10{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724909} {:answer/id #uuid "91d20f4a-7253-5bb2-ba08-4d8e9421fc1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=360+10y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724916} {:answer/id #uuid "bf260890-4cfc-5b78-a562-59722249897d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=4{y}^{2}-224" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724927} {:answer/id #uuid "e361bb8e-51ee-507a-b4b0-cd202d84139c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-4{y}^{2}+168" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724931}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724908} :answer-field/id #uuid "42cc042c-9b4b-5763-a07a-6d7cac56becf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724905}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :