17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "542bc4b8-7db6-5eeb-b34e-4fb6af139a5f" :question/problem "Given that $y>30$ and $t>0$, which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited decay model has a constant positive proportionality factor and approaches its equilibrium from above:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\\qquad k>0,\\qquad y>a\n$$\n\nRewrite the selected equation without changing its value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dt}&=8(30-y) \\\\ &=-8(y-30)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $k=8>0$ and the equilibrium is $a=30$. The prompt supplies $y>30$, which places the solution above its equilibrium.\n\nThe remaining alternatives have a positive linear coefficient or a variable factor or power that prevents a model linear in the displacement from equilibrium."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724952 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2daa9dac-2b79-5a22-8e40-e3354fd2c8c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-15y(8-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724937} {:answer/id #uuid "8186af10-6089-5b4e-8624-57920d13d8fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=3(60-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724942} {:answer/id #uuid "99de18f4-e8d1-5110-b514-dcdcf1ac2379" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=4y(y+30)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724944} {:answer/id #uuid "cf408d0d-2219-51d9-92a5-030caa03ac94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=8(y+30)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724951} {:answer/id #uuid "f900310d-2cb1-5b1c-a6e5-8fbdb9942ad2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=8(y-30)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724955}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724942} :answer-field/id #uuid "11208f86-8f1f-583a-bd93-f4c95bab2c79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724936}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d1d6d818-511c-59aa-ba4e-9db46eb6227b" :question/problem "Given that $y>60$ and $t>0$, which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited decay model has a constant positive proportionality factor and approaches its equilibrium from above:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\\qquad k>0,\\qquad y>a\n$$\n\nRewrite the selected equation without changing its value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dt}&=3(60-y) \\\\ &=-3(y-60)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $k=3>0$ and the equilibrium is $a=60$. The prompt supplies $y>60$, which places the solution above its equilibrium.\n\nThe remaining alternatives have a positive linear coefficient or a variable factor or power that prevents a model linear in the displacement from equilibrium."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724953 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b426880-0d1b-5d15-a77a-28e2f8b986c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5(35-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724941} {:answer/id #uuid "90310f04-8cb8-5b37-885f-10d62b413070" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-2y(y+41)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724943} {:answer/id #uuid "b5022d40-3fb4-5087-a921-808ca493cdba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-2({y}^{2}-41)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724947} {:answer/id #uuid "c21c92c9-23fd-5639-9c77-6412f6786cf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-10{y}^{2}(y-32)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724949} {:answer/id #uuid "e7c07fc9-757d-55d9-a58b-bae9aa6e6f16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-4(y-20)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724954}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724954} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a96f405-4e0a-5140-ae60-0ae129c62bcd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724939}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d5ac1772-880e-5554-9583-9501dca71d85" :question/problem "Given that $y>20$ and $t>0$, which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited decay model has a constant positive proportionality factor and approaches its equilibrium from above:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\\qquad k>0,\\qquad y>a\n$$\n\nThe selected equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-4(y-20)\n$$\n\nThus $k=4>0$ and the equilibrium is $a=20$. The prompt supplies $y>20$, which places the solution above its equilibrium.\n\nThe remaining alternatives have a positive linear coefficient or a variable factor or power that prevents a model linear in the displacement from equilibrium."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725264 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dff7f3b-f04f-52ff-8b65-1d11a8f07fa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x\\,\\left({x}^{3} + \\left(-4\\right)\\,{y}^{3}\\right) + 1}{x\\,\\left({x}^{2} + 4\\,{y}^{2}\\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725255} {:answer/id #uuid "1fb6be80-b9e1-5340-abe6-540e5af8b362" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y\\,\\left({x}^{3} + 4\\,{y}^{3}\\right)}{y\\,\\left(x + 2\\,y\\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725257} {:answer/id #uuid "2cc0462d-1283-5279-95be-318a45d1e834" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x\\,\\left({x}^{4} + 4\\,{y}^{4}\\right)}{x\\,\\left({x}^{2} + 3\\,{y}^{2}\\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725260} {:answer/id #uuid "442aa357-71f5-52d8-822f-d859ad28c159" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x\\,\\left(x + 2\\,y\\right)}{x\\,\\left(x + 4\\,y\\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725266} {:answer/id #uuid "7b687fd7-ac0f-5346-b972-20351cf1c3c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x\\,\\left({x}^{2} + \\left(-3\\right)\\,{y}^{2}\\right)}{x\\,\\left(x + y\\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725271}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725266} :answer-field/id #uuid "40de73e6-7093-5070-8ba8-5f78674640a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725265}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3e46fe05-5ce1-5dfe-b2b0-4effa92da6e3" :question/problem "For $x>0$ and $y>0$, wherever the displayed expressions are defined, which of the following is a first-order homogeneous ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "Write each equation as $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=f(x,y)$. It is homogeneous precisely when $f(tx,ty)=f(x,y)$ for every $t>0$ on its domain. A function of nonzero degree does not qualify.\n\n**Equation**\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x\\,\\left({x}^{4} + 4\\,{y}^{4}\\right)}{x\\,\\left({x}^{2} + 3\\,{y}^{2}\\right)}\n$$\n\nLet $f(x,y)$ be its right-hand side. The numerator degree minus the denominator degree is $2$, so a factor $t^{2}$ remains.\n\nUnder scaling, $f(tx,ty)={t}^{2}\\,\\frac{x\\,\\left({x}^{4} + 4\\,{y}^{4}\\right)}{x\\,\\left({x}^{2} + 3\\,{y}^{2}\\right)}$. This equation is **not** homogeneous.\n\n**Equation**\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x\\,\\left(x + 2\\,y\\right)}{x\\,\\left(x + 4\\,y\\right)}\n$$\n\nLet $f(x,y)$ be its right-hand side. Numerator and denominator have the same total degree $2$. Their scaling factors cancel.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty)&=\\frac{t\\,x\\,\\left(t\\,x + 2\\,t\\,y\\right)}{t\\,x\\,\\left(t\\,x + 4\\,t\\,y\\right)}\\\\ &=\\frac{x\\,\\left(x + 2\\,y\\right)}{x\\,\\left(x + 4\\,y\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$ This equation is homogeneous.\n\n**Equation**\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y\\,\\left({x}^{3} + 4\\,{y}^{3}\\right)}{y\\,\\left(x + 2\\,y\\right)}\n$$\n\nLet $f(x,y)$ be its right-hand side. The numerator degree minus the denominator degree is $2$, so a factor $t^{2}$ remains.\n\nUnder scaling, $f(tx,ty)={t}^{2}\\,\\frac{y\\,\\left({x}^{3} + 4\\,{y}^{3}\\right)}{y\\,\\left(x + 2\\,y\\right)}$. This equation is **not** homogeneous.\n\n**Equation**\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x\\,\\left({x}^{2} + \\left(-3\\right)\\,{y}^{2}\\right)}{x\\,\\left(x + y\\right)}\n$$\n\nLet $f(x,y)$ be its right-hand side. The numerator degree minus the denominator degree is $1$, so a factor $t^{1}$ remains.\n\nUnder scaling, $f(tx,ty)=t\\,\\frac{x\\,\\left({x}^{2} + \\left(-3\\right)\\,{y}^{2}\\right)}{x\\,\\left(x + y\\right)}$. This equation is **not** homogeneous.\n\n**Equation**\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x\\,\\left({x}^{3} + \\left(-4\\right)\\,{y}^{3}\\right) + 1}{x\\,\\left({x}^{2} + 4\\,{y}^{2}\\right)}\n$$\n\nLet $f(x,y)$ be its right-hand side. The added nonzero constant scales differently from the other numerator terms and cannot cancel against this homogeneous denominator.\n\nUnder scaling, $f(tx,ty)=\\frac{{t}^{4}\\,x\\,\\left({x}^{3} + \\left(-4\\right)\\,{y}^{3}\\right) + 1}{{t}^{3}\\,x\\,\\left({x}^{2} + 4\\,{y}^{2}\\right)}$. This equation is **not** homogeneous.\n\nTherefore select\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x\\,\\left(x + 2\\,y\\right)}{x\\,\\left(x + 4\\,y\\right)}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725275 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09fb6ad5-2d42-5863-9321-945b5422bd91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3\\,{x}^{2} + {y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725252} {:answer/id #uuid "70d85d29-992b-5478-9480-518c3cca8125" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2\\,x}{3\\,{x}^{2} + {y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725269} {:answer/id #uuid "739d0828-49d6-57d7-b2e7-7cf82cf21852" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3\\,{x}^{2} + {y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725270} {:answer/id #uuid "90eecb2a-b5fe-56d9-b991-fcf300b37d95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{3\\,{x}^{2} + {y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725274} {:answer/id #uuid "c3549f81-6543-53de-8e63-df8f5cf85348" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{3} + 2}{2\\,{x}^{2} + 4\\,{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725281}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725270} :answer-field/id #uuid "87445901-b14c-59b6-ac54-4b4c115e40c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9195e8a0-aff1-52bc-935d-db02a4717485" :question/problem "For $x>0$ and $y>0$, wherever the displayed expressions are defined, which of the following is a first-order homogeneous ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "Write each equation as $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=f(x,y)$. It is homogeneous precisely when $f(tx,ty)=f(x,y)$ for every $t>0$ on its domain. A function of nonzero degree does not qualify.\n\n**Equation**\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3\\,{x}^{2} + {y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nLet $f(x,y)$ be its right-hand side. Numerator and denominator have the same total degree $2$. Their scaling factors cancel.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty)&=\\frac{\\left(t\\,x\\right)^{2}}{3\\,\\left(t\\,x\\right)^{2} + \\left(t\\,y\\right)^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3\\,{x}^{2} + {y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$ This equation is homogeneous.\n\n**Equation**\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2\\,x}{3\\,{x}^{2} + {y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nLet $f(x,y)$ be its right-hand side. The numerator degree minus the denominator degree is $-1$, so a factor $t^{-1}$ remains.\n\nUnder scaling, $f(tx,ty)={t}^{-1}\\,\\frac{2\\,x}{3\\,{x}^{2} + {y}^{2}}$. This equation is **not** homogeneous.\n\n**Equation**\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{3\\,{x}^{2} + {y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nLet $f(x,y)$ be its right-hand side. The numerator degree minus the denominator degree is $2$, so a factor $t^{2}$ remains.\n\nUnder scaling, $f(tx,ty)={t}^{2}\\,\\frac{{x}^{4}}{3\\,{x}^{2} + {y}^{2}}$. This equation is **not** homogeneous.\n\n**Equation**\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3\\,{x}^{2} + {y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nLet $f(x,y)$ be its right-hand side. The numerator degree minus the denominator degree is $1$, so a factor $t^{1}$ remains.\n\nUnder scaling, $f(tx,ty)=t\\,\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3\\,{x}^{2} + {y}^{2}}$. This equation is **not** homogeneous.\n\n**Equation**\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{3} + 2}{2\\,{x}^{2} + 4\\,{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nLet $f(x,y)$ be its right-hand side. The added nonzero constant scales differently from the other numerator terms and cannot cancel against this homogeneous denominator.\n\nUnder scaling, $f(tx,ty)=\\frac{{t}^{3}\\,{y}^{3} + 2}{{t}^{2}\\,\\left(2\\,{x}^{2} + 4\\,{y}^{2}\\right)}$. This equation is **not** homogeneous.\n\nTherefore select\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3\\,{x}^{2} + {y}^{2}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725278 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b5a9292-9119-5eb4-9443-7d5798286058" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x\\,\\left({x}^{3} + 4\\,{y}^{3}\\right)}{x\\,\\left({x}^{2} + {y}^{2}\\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725262} {:answer/id #uuid "3e2465a0-4ec0-5bda-b9ce-e4cd9dc4f5e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x\\,\\left({x}^{3} + \\left(-3\\right)\\,{y}^{3}\\right)}{x\\,\\left({x}^{2} + 2\\,{y}^{2}\\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725263} {:answer/id #uuid "b13c632c-ba06-5d8b-89f2-b87816878390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y\\,\\left(1 + 2\\right) + 4}{y\\,\\left(x + 2\\,y\\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725279} {:answer/id #uuid "ed39ae38-423a-5f82-82c6-91b546fd4635" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y\\,\\left({x}^{2} + \\left(-2\\right)\\,{y}^{2}\\right)}{y\\,\\left(x + 3\\,y\\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725284} {:answer/id #uuid "fd4885bb-aa16-5585-904d-1f9f4b8f5351" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y\\,\\left({x}^{2} + \\left(-3\\right)\\,{y}^{2}\\right)}{y\\,\\left({x}^{2} + {y}^{2}\\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725286}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725286} :answer-field/id #uuid "2baf3220-4536-59dd-818a-1b5a6a7cc4c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725259}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a58c32d9-9c9e-5e7f-bfa9-ca043676c7a3" :question/problem "For $x>0$ and $y>0$, wherever the displayed expressions are defined, which of the following is a first-order homogeneous ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "Write each equation as $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=f(x,y)$. It is homogeneous precisely when $f(tx,ty)=f(x,y)$ for every $t>0$ on its domain. A function of nonzero degree does not qualify.\n\n**Equation**\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y\\,\\left({x}^{2} + \\left(-2\\right)\\,{y}^{2}\\right)}{y\\,\\left(x + 3\\,y\\right)}\n$$\n\nLet $f(x,y)$ be its right-hand side. The numerator degree minus the denominator degree is $1$, so a factor $t^{1}$ remains.\n\nUnder scaling, $f(tx,ty)=t\\,\\frac{y\\,\\left({x}^{2} + \\left(-2\\right)\\,{y}^{2}\\right)}{y\\,\\left(x + 3\\,y\\right)}$. This equation is **not** homogeneous.\n\n**Equation**\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x\\,\\left({x}^{3} + \\left(-3\\right)\\,{y}^{3}\\right)}{x\\,\\left({x}^{2} + 2\\,{y}^{2}\\right)}\n$$\n\nLet $f(x,y)$ be its right-hand side. The numerator degree minus the denominator degree is $1$, so a factor $t^{1}$ remains.\n\nUnder scaling, $f(tx,ty)=t\\,\\frac{x\\,\\left({x}^{3} + \\left(-3\\right)\\,{y}^{3}\\right)}{x\\,\\left({x}^{2} + 2\\,{y}^{2}\\right)}$. This equation is **not** homogeneous.\n\n**Equation**\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y\\,\\left({x}^{2} + \\left(-3\\right)\\,{y}^{2}\\right)}{y\\,\\left({x}^{2} + {y}^{2}\\right)}\n$$\n\nLet $f(x,y)$ be its right-hand side. Numerator and denominator have the same total degree $3$. Their scaling factors cancel.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty)&=\\frac{t\\,y\\,\\left(\\left(t\\,x\\right)^{2} + \\left(-3\\right)\\,\\left(t\\,y\\right)^{2}\\right)}{t\\,y\\,\\left(\\left(t\\,x\\right)^{2} + \\left(t\\,y\\right)^{2}\\right)}\\\\ &=\\frac{y\\,\\left({x}^{2} + \\left(-3\\right)\\,{y}^{2}\\right)}{y\\,\\left({x}^{2} + {y}^{2}\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$ This equation is homogeneous.\n\n**Equation**\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y\\,\\left(1 + 2\\right) + 4}{y\\,\\left(x + 2\\,y\\right)}\n$$\n\nLet $f(x,y)$ be its right-hand side. The added nonzero constant scales differently from the other numerator terms and cannot cancel against this homogeneous denominator.\n\nUnder scaling, $f(tx,ty)=\\frac{t\\,y\\,\\left(1 + 2\\right) + 4}{{t}^{2}\\,y\\,\\left(x + 2\\,y\\right)}$. This equation is **not** homogeneous.\n\n**Equation**\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x\\,\\left({x}^{3} + 4\\,{y}^{3}\\right)}{x\\,\\left({x}^{2} + {y}^{2}\\right)}\n$$\n\nLet $f(x,y)$ be its right-hand side. The numerator degree minus the denominator degree is $1$, so a factor $t^{1}$ remains.\n\nUnder scaling, $f(tx,ty)=t\\,\\frac{x\\,\\left({x}^{3} + 4\\,{y}^{3}\\right)}{x\\,\\left({x}^{2} + {y}^{2}\\right)}$. This equation is **not** homogeneous.\n\nTherefore select\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y\\,\\left({x}^{2} + \\left(-3\\right)\\,{y}^{2}\\right)}{y\\,\\left({x}^{2} + {y}^{2}\\right)}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725283 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37f10c2e-41e5-5032-a5d7-89000252eb66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2\\,{x}^{4} + {y}^{4}}{{x}^{2} + 3\\,{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725261} {:answer/id #uuid "5ca30c0f-9285-52e4-a023-13302c82d98c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{4\\,{x}^{3} + 2\\,{y}^{3} + 3}{{x}^{2} + {y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725267} {:answer/id #uuid "604ff966-57e0-5ea7-a6a3-fce51d6d2243" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2\\,{x}^{3} + 4\\,{y}^{3}}{{x}^{2} + 3\\,{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725268} {:answer/id #uuid "7cb84b31-8256-52b7-bc7e-587fce19581d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{x}^{2} + \\left(-3\\right)\\,{y}^{2}}{2\\,{x}^{2} + 4\\,{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725272} {:answer/id #uuid "e43c3c61-43e8-5632-8965-7e19495fc0bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2\\,x + y}{{x}^{2} + 2\\,{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725282}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725272} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bbeff41-1d7d-5445-ab99-6a414ea9a5fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725276}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e719a4c7-fca6-59e2-b12d-85968def0d08" :question/problem "For $x>0$ and $y>0$, wherever the displayed expressions are defined, which of the following is a first-order homogeneous ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "Write each equation as $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=f(x,y)$. It is homogeneous precisely when $f(tx,ty)=f(x,y)$ for every $t>0$ on its domain. A function of nonzero degree does not qualify.\n\n**Equation**\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{x}^{2} + \\left(-3\\right)\\,{y}^{2}}{2\\,{x}^{2} + 4\\,{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nLet $f(x,y)$ be its right-hand side. Numerator and denominator have the same total degree $2$. Their scaling factors cancel.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty)&=\\frac{\\left(t\\,x\\right)^{2} + \\left(-3\\right)\\,\\left(t\\,y\\right)^{2}}{2\\,\\left(t\\,x\\right)^{2} + 4\\,\\left(t\\,y\\right)^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{{x}^{2} + \\left(-3\\right)\\,{y}^{2}}{2\\,{x}^{2} + 4\\,{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$ This equation is homogeneous.\n\n**Equation**\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2\\,{x}^{4} + {y}^{4}}{{x}^{2} + 3\\,{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nLet $f(x,y)$ be its right-hand side. The numerator degree minus the denominator degree is $2$, so a factor $t^{2}$ remains.\n\nUnder scaling, $f(tx,ty)={t}^{2}\\,\\frac{2\\,{x}^{4} + {y}^{4}}{{x}^{2} + 3\\,{y}^{2}}$. This equation is **not** homogeneous.\n\n**Equation**\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2\\,x + y}{{x}^{2} + 2\\,{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nLet $f(x,y)$ be its right-hand side. The numerator degree minus the denominator degree is $-1$, so a factor $t^{-1}$ remains.\n\nUnder scaling, $f(tx,ty)={t}^{-1}\\,\\frac{2\\,x + y}{{x}^{2} + 2\\,{y}^{2}}$. This equation is **not** homogeneous.\n\n**Equation**\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2\\,{x}^{3} + 4\\,{y}^{3}}{{x}^{2} + 3\\,{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nLet $f(x,y)$ be its right-hand side. The numerator degree minus the denominator degree is $1$, so a factor $t^{1}$ remains.\n\nUnder scaling, $f(tx,ty)=t\\,\\frac{2\\,{x}^{3} + 4\\,{y}^{3}}{{x}^{2} + 3\\,{y}^{2}}$. This equation is **not** homogeneous.\n\n**Equation**\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{4\\,{x}^{3} + 2\\,{y}^{3} + 3}{{x}^{2} + {y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nLet $f(x,y)$ be its right-hand side. The added nonzero constant scales differently from the other numerator terms and cannot cancel against this homogeneous denominator.\n\nUnder scaling, $f(tx,ty)=\\frac{{t}^{3}\\,\\left(4\\,{x}^{3} + 2\\,{y}^{3}\\right) + 3}{{t}^{2}\\,\\left({x}^{2} + {y}^{2}\\right)}$. This equation is **not** homogeneous.\n\nTherefore select\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{x}^{2} + \\left(-3\\right)\\,{y}^{2}}{2\\,{x}^{2} + 4\\,{y}^{2}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725285 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ca941f3-71dc-525a-b289-9223b69896ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{3\\,{x}^{3} + \\left(-4\\right)\\,{y}^{3}}{2\\,{x}^{3} + 2\\,{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725253} {:answer/id #uuid "0dd64e6f-badc-585a-b266-7b251da8a464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{4\\,{x}^{5} + 2\\,{y}^{5}}{{x}^{3} + 4\\,{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725254} {:answer/id #uuid "1e5cf88a-c391-5a35-8391-3ec7977b2d97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{3\\,{x}^{4} + {y}^{4}}{4\\,{x}^{3} + 2\\,{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725256} {:answer/id #uuid "a3f9baeb-cddf-5e90-b37f-39686057e564" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{4\\,{x}^{4} + \\left(-1\\right)\\,{y}^{4}}{{x}^{3} + 2\\,{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725277} {:answer/id #uuid "bb34f667-8389-5934-919c-4dfde4b883fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{x}^{2} + 3\\,{y}^{2}}{2\\,{x}^{3} + 2\\,{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725280}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725253} :answer-field/id #uuid "25320ab1-4f69-5b45-8097-692c705f3240" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f782e794-987c-539b-b137-cb61db0ec762" :question/problem "For $x>0$ and $y>0$, wherever the displayed expressions are defined, which of the following is a first-order homogeneous ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "Write each equation as $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=f(x,y)$. It is homogeneous precisely when $f(tx,ty)=f(x,y)$ for every $t>0$ on its domain. A function of nonzero degree does not qualify.\n\n**Equation**\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{4\\,{x}^{4} + \\left(-1\\right)\\,{y}^{4}}{{x}^{3} + 2\\,{y}^{3}}\n$$\n\nLet $f(x,y)$ be its right-hand side. The numerator degree minus the denominator degree is $1$, so a factor $t^{1}$ remains.\n\nUnder scaling, $f(tx,ty)=t\\,\\frac{4\\,{x}^{4} + \\left(-1\\right)\\,{y}^{4}}{{x}^{3} + 2\\,{y}^{3}}$. This equation is **not** homogeneous.\n\n**Equation**\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{4\\,{x}^{5} + 2\\,{y}^{5}}{{x}^{3} + 4\\,{y}^{3}}\n$$\n\nLet $f(x,y)$ be its right-hand side. The numerator degree minus the denominator degree is $2$, so a factor $t^{2}$ remains.\n\nUnder scaling, $f(tx,ty)={t}^{2}\\,\\frac{4\\,{x}^{5} + 2\\,{y}^{5}}{{x}^{3} + 4\\,{y}^{3}}$. This equation is **not** homogeneous.\n\n**Equation**\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{x}^{2} + 3\\,{y}^{2}}{2\\,{x}^{3} + 2\\,{y}^{3}}\n$$\n\nLet $f(x,y)$ be its right-hand side. The numerator degree minus the denominator degree is $-1$, so a factor $t^{-1}$ remains.\n\nUnder scaling, $f(tx,ty)={t}^{-1}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2} + 3\\,{y}^{2}}{2\\,{x}^{3} + 2\\,{y}^{3}}$. This equation is **not** homogeneous.\n\n**Equation**\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{3\\,{x}^{3} + \\left(-4\\right)\\,{y}^{3}}{2\\,{x}^{3} + 2\\,{y}^{3}}\n$$\n\nLet $f(x,y)$ be its right-hand side. Numerator and denominator have the same total degree $3$. Their scaling factors cancel.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty)&=\\frac{3\\,\\left(t\\,x\\right)^{3} + \\left(-4\\right)\\,\\left(t\\,y\\right)^{3}}{2\\,\\left(t\\,x\\right)^{3} + 2\\,\\left(t\\,y\\right)^{3}}\\\\ &=\\frac{3\\,{x}^{3} + \\left(-4\\right)\\,{y}^{3}}{2\\,{x}^{3} + 2\\,{y}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$ This equation is homogeneous.\n\n**Equation**\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{3\\,{x}^{4} + {y}^{4}}{4\\,{x}^{3} + 2\\,{y}^{3}}\n$$\n\nLet $f(x,y)$ be its right-hand side. The numerator degree minus the denominator degree is $1$, so a factor $t^{1}$ remains.\n\nUnder scaling, $f(tx,ty)=t\\,\\frac{3\\,{x}^{4} + {y}^{4}}{4\\,{x}^{3} + 2\\,{y}^{3}}$. This equation is **not** homogeneous.\n\nTherefore select\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{3\\,{x}^{3} + \\left(-4\\right)\\,{y}^{3}}{2\\,{x}^{3} + 2\\,{y}^{3}}.\n$$"}]}
